WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:05.220
We are now ready to start
sequencing real spectra.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.920
But before we do this,

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:11.768
we need to agree on how to score
a peptide against a spectrum.

00:00:11.768 --> 00:00:18.635
After transforming both the peptide in
spectrum into peptide vector and

00:00:18.635 --> 00:00:23.195
spectrum vector, it will be easy
because this peptide vector and

00:00:23.195 --> 00:00:27.360
spectrum vector will have
the same dimension, and thus,

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.490
we can score a peptide against
spectrum as simply the dot product of

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.570
the peptide vector and the spectrum vector.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:40.720
And therefore, the Peptide Sequencing
Problem can be defined as follows:

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.660
Given a spectral vector,
find a peptide vector

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:49.150
with a maximum score
against this spectral vector.

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.380
The input to this problem
is a spectral vector and

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:58.130
the output is an amino acid string Peptide
that maximizes the score between Peptide and

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:02.050
Spectrum among all possible peptides.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:07.890
To solve this problem, we will
construct a directed acyclic graph for

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.610
a given spectral vector.

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:13.916
For a spectral vector &lt;s_1, ..., s_m&gt;,

00:01:13.916 --> 00:01:18.283
we will construct a graph
on m+1 nodes,

00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:22.320
with these nodes labeled as 0, 1, ...,  m.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.240
And we will assign weight s_i to node i.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.930
How will we construct
the edges in this graph?

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:33.460
We will connect the node i to the node j,

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:39.480
if j-i is equal to
the mass of an amino acid.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:45.320
In our two alphabet of two amino acids
x and z these masses 4 and 5,

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:50.470
this will be the set of edges
corresponding to amino acid x,

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:55.050
and this will be the set of edges
corresponding to amino acid y.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:02.570
And the score between Peptide and
Spectrum will be defined as simply

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:07.770
sum of scores or
weights of nodes in this path.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.460
In this case it will be 22.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:14.810
The conclusion from this
construction is that

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:19.530
every peptide corresponds
to a path from source to sink

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:23.420
in the constructed DAG, and the
score between Peptide and

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:29.620
Spectrum is simply the sum of scores
of the nodes this path visits.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:34.560
And therefore it would just reframed the
Peptide Sequencing Problem as the problem

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.340
of finding a maximum weight path
in a node-weighted graph.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:46.930
We already know how to find the maximum
paths in the edge-weighted graph,

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.000
and it will be a good exercise for

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.860
you to try to modify the algorithm
that we previously learned for

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:58.722
edge-weighted graphs into an algorithm
for node-weighted graphs.

00:02:58.722 --> 00:03:02.842
We are now ready to attack
the problem of finding

00:03:02.842 --> 00:03:07.731
an unknown peptide that
generated a given spectrum.

00:03:07.731 --> 00:03:12.466
This is the spectrum that was
generated by a mass spectrometer from

00:03:12.466 --> 00:03:17.750
an unknown peptide, and
our goal is to solve the reverse problem:

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:22.680
Given a spectrum, find what was
the peptide that generated this spectrum.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:28.777
After we learned about spectral vectors, it
looks like the solution of this problem

00:03:28.777 --> 00:03:30.070
will be easy.

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:35.330
Indeed, it may appear that
the only thing we need

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:40.360
to do is to simply find the De Novo
Reconstruction of the spectrum, or

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:45.799
to find the peptide with the highest
score against this spectrum.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:50.520
Before, we of course, will transform
the spectrum into a spectral vector.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:52.860
In this case,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:58.130
the solution of this peptide sequencing
problem will generate this peptide that,

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:00.610
as it turns out, is incorrect.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:07.450
The reason it is incorrect is that,
despite two decades of research,

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:11.820
scoring functions that reliably
assign the highest score

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.870
to the biologically correct
peptide remain unknown.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:20.990
It looks like we've put
ourselves into a corner.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:27.120
We learned how to sequence peptides as
the highest scoring peptide, but,

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.700
as it turns out, this highest-scoring
peptide is not biologically relevant.

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:39.100
However, imagine for a second that
you know the proteome of the species.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:43.310
If you know the proteome,
what is the point of finding

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:47.920
the highest scoring peptide
among all peptides?

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:52.640
Shouldn't we instead find the highest
scoring peptide in the proteome?

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:58.610
And, it turns out that,

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:03.520
although the correct peptide may not
score highest among all peptides,

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:08.710
it typically scores highest among
all peptides in the proteome.

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:16.110
There is a small print here and this
small print says "If the resulting score is

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:20.880
sufficiently high", and we will talk more
about this important condition later, but

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:27.930
it looks like we reduced problem
of interpreting the spectrum to

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:33.150
peptide identification rather than peptide
sequencing, which is reconstructing

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:38.860
a peptide as the highest-scoring
peptide occurring in a proteome.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:44.850
If you compare peptide sequencing
with peptide identification,

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.729
you will find something quite surprising.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:52.636
And as we saw in Peptide Sequencing,
we defined the best scoring

00:05:52.636 --> 00:05:57.385
peptide as simply the longest
path in the constructed DAG.

00:05:57.385 --> 00:06:00.691
On the other hand,
in Peptide Identification,

00:06:00.691 --> 00:06:06.590
we find the solution by simply finding the
highest-scoring peptide in the proteome.

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:12.227
Hopefully, these two solutions
will result in the same peptide,

00:06:12.227 --> 00:06:14.730
but it is often not the case.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:20.325
Interestingly, Peptide Sequencing is
not unlike Peptide Identification.

00:06:20.325 --> 00:06:25.395
In the case when the proteome consists
of all possible peptides, let's say

00:06:25.395 --> 00:06:30.145
in the case of peptides of length n,
it will be 20^n peptides.

00:06:32.535 --> 00:06:35.074
What approach do you think is faster,

00:06:35.074 --> 00:06:37.781
Peptide Identification or
Peptide Sequencing?

00:06:37.781 --> 00:06:42.914
It looks like Peptide
Identification should be faster

00:06:42.914 --> 00:06:48.580
because the size of the space we
have to explore is much smaller.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:55.590
However, the reality is that peptide
sequencing algorithms are much faster,

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:59.520
even though their search
space is much larger.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:04.970
The reason is that peptide sequencing
eliminates the time-consuming scan of

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:09.960
proteome by modeling the problem as
the longest path in the DAG problem.

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:15.030
However, since the scoring
function is imperfect,

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:17.305
peptide sequencing remains inaccurate.

00:07:17.305 --> 00:07:19.931
State-of-the-art algorithms for

00:07:19.931 --> 00:07:24.530
peptide sequencing only identify
30% of peptides correctly.

